\n                if (null != mListener) {\n                    // Notify the active callbacks interface (the activity, if the\n                    // fragment is attached to one) that an item has been selected.\n                    mListener.onListFragmentInteraction(holder.mItem);\n                }\n            }\n        });\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public int getItemCount() {\n        return mValues.size();\n    }\n\n    public class ViewHolder extends RecyclerView.ViewHolder {\n        public final View mView;\n        public final TextView mContentView;\n        public Schedule mItem;\n\n        public ViewHolder(View view) {\n            super(view);\n            mView = view;\n            mContentView = (TextView) view.findViewById(R.id.content);\n        }\n\n        @Override\n        public String toString() {\n            return super.toString() + \" '\" + mContentView.getText() + \"'\";\n        }\n    }\n}\n"}]}}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"d46436ad-6f8e-463c-b741-982fb0800109","session_id":"5e3a2bf1-94b8-4b97-9178-7a94fbc6e2eb","parent_tool_use_id":null,"message":{"id":"d46436ad-6f8e-463c-b741-982fb0800109","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"qwen/qwen3.6-35b-a3b","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a complete picture. Let me also check if the training type is displayed in the Schedule view. Let me also check the fragment_schedule.xml to see what's displayed for each schedule.\n\nLet me summarize the bugs I've found:\n\n**Bug 1: `GeneralTrainingScheduleEditor.java` - The TrainingsType Spinner is not being read**\nIn `content_general_training_schedule_editor.xml`, there's a `Spinner` with id `trainSpinner` that lets users select a training type (strength endurance, hypertrophy, maximum strength, pyramid training). However, in `GeneralTrainingScheduleEditor.java`, the `addScheduleToModel()` method:\n1. Creates a new `Schedule` object\n\n\n2. Sets repetitions, pause, sets, and speed from their respective fields, but never reads from the `trainSpinner` or calls `setTrainingsType()` to persist the user's selection.\n\n**Bug 2: `XMLParser.java` - Training type is not being serialized/deserialized**\nThe `SCHEDULE_TRAININGSTYPE_TAG` constant exists but the code to read and write it is commented out in both `parseFile()` and `writeFile()`, so even if the training type were set, it wouldn't be saved to or loaded from the XML file.\n\nFor Bug 1, I need to set up an `OnItemSelectedListener` on the `trainSpinner` to capture the selected training type and pass it to `schedule.setTrainingsType()` when the schedule is created. I need to wire up the spinner to capture the user's training type selection and pass it to the schedule object. I can use either an OnItemSelectedListener or read the value directly when the form is submitted, then map the selected string from the array to the corresponding enum value, making sure to handle the underscore formatting difference between the XML array items and the enum constants. I'm realizing the enum naming convention has underscores, but `name()` outputs them as-is while the string array uses spaces, so I can't directly use `valueOf()`. I need to convert the selected item by replacing spaces with underscores to match the enum names. I'm realizing that `valueOf(\"strength_endurance\")` expects exactly \"strength_endurance\", not \"strength endurance\" with a space, so `Enum.valueOf()` won't work directly. I need a custom conversion function to map the formatted string from the spinner to the correct enum value—either by replacing spaces with underscores or using a lookup approach. I'm checking whether `name().replace('_', ' ')` correctly converts the enum values back to their display format—for `strength_endurance`, it would become \"strength endurance\", which should match what's in the string array. So I can create a lookup method that maps display names to enum values using a HashMap, then retrieve the selected type from the spinner and set it on the schedule object.\n\nBut actually, a simpler approach is to just transform the selected text by converting it to lowercase, replacing spaces with underscores, and wrapping it in `TrainingsTypes.valueOf()`. I just need to import `TrainingsTypes` from the model package, and since the spinner displays text that directly corresponds to the enum values, this should work reliably.\n\nNow I'm mapping out all three fixes: wiring up the spinner in the editor activity to actually save the training type, and uncommenting the relevant lines in the XML parser to handle serialization and deserialization of that field.\n\nFor the first fix, I need to wire up the spinner to capture the selected training type when the user makes a choice, converting the display text to the enum format. The second and third fixes are straightforward—just uncommenting the existing code that already handles the conversion correctly.\n\nI'm also verifying that the spinner items in the string array match the enum names when underscores replace spaces, so the conversion should work cleanly. Now I'll apply these changes to the code.\n","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0}}}
